5,Okwy Osadebe and Highlife Soundmakers - Egwu Mmanwụ Igbo (Music of Igbo Masquerade) | Highlife Music from Nigeria | PALENQUE RECORDS,https://palenquerecords.bandcamp.com/album/okwy-osadebe-and-highlife-soundmakers-egwu-mmanw-igbo-music-of-igbo-masquerade,"September 13, 2024 - Chief Osadebe thus, introduced a unique traditional folkloric sound of the River Niger waves with horns and guitar driven rhythms. He borrowed heavy melody riffs from the traditional music, propelled each song with the vernacular Igbo dialect, ...",Ogbia,Niger-Congo
6,Highlife & The (Igbo) Gospel: The Revised Congolese Version | by E. Egbejule | Medium,https://medium.com/@EromoEgbejule/highlife-the-igbo-gospel-the-revised-congolese-version-b1a20ea34d2a,"May 5, 2018 - Highlife & The (Igbo) Gospel: The Revised Congolese Version From Onitsha to Kinshasa… In order to address the relationship between Congolese music especially the genres of soukous/Makossa/mapouka …",Ogbia,Niger-Congo
7,Best of 2017: Ogene: How an Igbo genre broke into mainstream Nigerian music | Music In Africa,https://www.musicinafrica.net/magazine/best-2017-ogene-how-igbo-genre-broke-mainstream-nigerian-music,"December 27, 2017 - To enjoy ogene, one doesn't only listen to the music; you watch a performance, as the sound lends itself to drama and robust dance. A typical ogene performance is atilogwu without calisthenics. And today, in a little diluted form, it has passed into mainstream Nigerian music – thanks to songs like Zoro's ‘Ogene’, ‘Achikolo’ and Flavour's ‘Gbo Gan Gbom’. And, truth be told, the sound has paid its dues with sweat, grime and blood.",Ogbia,Niger-Congo
8,Has Nigerian Afrobeats overtaken Congolese rumba?,https://www.theafricareport.com/5434/has-nigerian-afrobeats-overtaken-congolese-rumba/,"August 9, 2013 - For years Congolese rumba was a continental favourite, but now West African tunes are getting young people dancing from Nairobi to Abidjan. ... YesI think the latest Afrobeats music is more urbanised. It is what is trending at the moment and is aimed at a younger generation, and they are the future.",Ogbia,Niger-Congo
